      "analyses": [
        {
          "id": "williams_flats_rise",
          "description": "Rise of the Williams Flats plume over the first 30 minutes of the fire: plume-top height and maximum vertical velocity. The plume reaches a quasi-steady height of about 8.5 km with a pyrocumulus cloud above about 4 km.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0,
            "end": 1800
          },
          "plots": [
            {
              "id": "plume_height",
              "type": "line",
              "x": {
                "variable": "t",
                "label": "Time (s)"
              },
              "y": {
                "variable": "hₚ",
                "label": "Plume top (m)"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "maximum_speed",
              "type": "line",
              "x": {
                "variable": "t",
                "label": "Time (s)"
              },
              "y": {
                "variable": "𝒲",
                "label": "Maximum vertical velocity (m/s)"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "williams_flats_continental",
          "description": "Williams Flats fire (2.56 kW/m2, 750 ha) with the default continental Berry autoconversion. At 300 s the plume is still dry and rising (top 1.3 km): vertical velocity, temperature excess, vapor and tracer near the source. At 1200 s the plume has reached 5.9 km and carries a pyrocumulus cloud above 3.5 km: cloud water through the cloud, and the vertical velocity, temperature excess, vapor and tracer through the column. Reference: the stand-alone Fortran (tartanrunner25/Plume-model_stand_alone at 9265514, GNU Fortran 11.5.0 -O3) with the Williams Flats inputs, built with the corrections listed as departures (1)-(4) and (7)-(10) (preprocessor flags FIX_PR FIX_SED FIX_SUBL FIX_LEAPFROG), its maximum time step reduced from 5 s to 0.2 s (DTMAX) and its sponge time fixed at 15 s (FIXED_SPONGE), so that it approximates the continuous-time limit this model represents; values are interpolated linearly in time between the two Fortran steps that bracket the assertion time and converted to the state units (temperature excess T - Te, water in g/kg, tracer as a fraction of the boundary value 1000). Tolerances are about twice the largest model-reference difference measured for each field over the asserted levels (one tolerance per field and test), with floors of 0.2 % (relative), 0.02 K and one grid level (100 m) for hp. Levels at the advancing plume head, where the reference itself changes by several per cent between 0.2 s and 0.1 s steps, are not asserted.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0,
            "end": 1200.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "hₚ",
              "time": 300.0,
              "expected": 1300.0,
              "tolerance": {
                "abs": 100.0
              }
            },
